  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1203 PM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nA heavy dome of high pressure remains over southwest CONUS today and \nis expected to throughout the next several days at least. There \nstill remains good moisture underneath the dome, with PWAT values \nlikely to get at or above 0.9-1.0\" today through the next several \ndays. This sets the stage for continued thunderstorm development \nover the high terrain early each afternoon, drifting off into the \nlower elevation areas mid to late afternoon. The threat for locally \nheavy rainfall remains with these storms, as they are likely to be \nslow moving with efficient rainfall rates. Areas in the Gila Region \nand the Sacramento Mountains are close to saturation based on \nsatellite-derived soil moisture, while areas from Las Cruces to \nDeming to El Paso and south along the Rio Grande are very dry. Both \nends of the soil moisture spectrum can lead to flooding issues with \nefficient rainfall rates should a storm form in these areas. While \nthere are low precipitation chances in the lowlands near the US-\nMexico border each day, a few colliding outflow boundaries in the \narea may develop an isolated storm (low confidence).\n\nAnother feature of this high pressure are the consistent hot \ntemperatures across the lower elevations. Repeated days of 100F or \ngreater high temperatures, combined with high 70s to even low 80s \nlow temperatures will increase the risk of heat-related impacts and \nillnesses. A Heat Advisory was issued for Monday for areas along the \nRio Grande Valley in our TX forecast areas, including the El Paso \nmetro area. There is rather high confidence that these areas will \nsee future Heat Advisories as well, given the high likelihood of \nthese temperatures persisting throughout the next several days.\n\nNot much change in the overall weather pattern is expected \nthroughout much of this week. The center of the high pressure is \nlikely to slowly meander its way westward towards AZ, but will not \ndo much to drastically change the conditions. Expecting the daily \nthunderstorm chances and hot temperatures to continue throughout the \nnext several days, just perhaps a slight change in storm motion each \nday.",
